        "text": "{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"reasons\":[\"Deviation is correctly identified: § 11.3 gives IBM an open-ended right to investigate Customer's use/Content with no frequency cap, no prior written notice (p0137 only requires an *attempted* notice, and only before 11.3(a)/(b) actions), no independent-auditor or confidentiality requirement, no limit on access to Customer records/systems and no cost allocation, with p0138 obliging Customer to hand over information on request — i.e. below the AUDIT preferred and fallback positions.\",\"Redline is valid and minimal: a single appended paragraph after p0137 that leaves existing text untouched and delivers all preferred elements (once per 12 months, 30 days' written notice, business hours, independent auditor under confidentiality, off-site where practicable with no direct system access, Vendor cost unless >5% underpayment). Scoping it to investigations 'that require access to Customer's records, facilities or systems' is a sensible narrowing that avoids blocking IBM's ability to act on its own hosted environment.\",\"Comment is accurate on substance, cites the playbook position and offers the 15-day/10% fallback in the required tone; the only slip is describing the current right as 'at our cost' when the original is simply silent on cost allocation — worth softening to 'with no cost allocation'.\",\"Refinements for the drafter (not fatal): (i) consider preserving § 11.3's existing emergency / required-by-law carve-out as an exception to the 30-day notice, since a flat 30-day requirement for suspected illegal-content investigations is likely to be rejected outright; (ii) limb (vi) imports a fee-audit 'underpayment exceeding 5% of amounts due for the period reviewed' concept into an acceptable-use investigation clause — retain the 'at IBM's expense' default but confirm the underpayment trigger is coherent here so it does not invite IBM to recharacterise 11.3 as a payment audit.\"]}"
      }
